<header><p class="enc-kicker">Work / monument · text</p><h1 class="enc-title">Lithuanian Metrica</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Lithuanian Metrica is the archive of the chancery of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/grand-duchy-of-lithuania">Grand Duchy of Lithuania</a>, consisting of copybooks of charters, court decisions, treaties and other records from the fifteenth to the eighteenth century. Most entries are in Ruthenian, with later ones in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/latin">Latin</a> and Polish. Taken to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/russia">Russia</a> after the partitions, most of it is now held in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/moscow">Moscow</a> and it is a major source for the history of the grand duchy.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/lithuanian-metrica.jpg" alt="Lithuanian Metrica" width="320" height="239" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Metryka_litewska.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>State archives were begun in the 13th-century Kingdom of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lithuania">Lithuania</a>, and diplomacy grew greatly under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gediminas">Gediminas</a>, but many documents were lost in wars, floods and city fires. The growing need to reproduce documents, and the mounting number of edicts, wills and court verdicts, led to the Metrica, kept systematically from the second half of the 15th century. It developed in parallel with, and on the model of, the Crown Metrica of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/poland">Poland</a>. The word comes from the Polish metryka, archive, and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/latin">Latin</a> matricula, office book.</p><p>The books were kept in Trakai Island Castle under the Treasurer until 1511, then in the Lower Castle of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vilnius">Vilnius</a> under the State Chancellor. In 1569, when <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/podlachia">Podlasie</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/volhynia">Volhynia</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/podolia">Podolia</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kyiv">Kiev</a> were joined to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/crown-of-the-kingdom-of-poland">Crown of the Kingdom of Poland</a>, the books concerning them went into the Crown Metrica. As the volumes were deteriorating, Grand Chancellor Lew Sapieha ordered them recopied in 1594, a task that lasted until 1607.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Contents</h2><p>At first the Metrica held copies of documents issued by the Grand Duke, the Lithuanian Council of Lords and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/seimas">Seimas</a>, together with some papers from outside, such as translations of the yarlyks of the khans of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/crimea">Crimea</a> and copies of Muscovy's diplomatic documents, and office records such as registers of acts. Later it took in transcripts of diplomatic correspondence, charters of privileges, wills, verdicts, judicial decrees and even some private letters received by officials. Its documents were to be preserved for ever, and it became the core of the Main State Archive of the grand duchy.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Discovery and study</h2><p>Large parts were lost in the wars with Muscovy, and Swedish armies carried books away in 1656–1657; many came back after the Treaty of Oliva (1660), though some were lost at sea on the way. The Metrica was taken to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/warsaw">Warsaw</a> in 1765, bound and catalogued, and after the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/third-partition-of-poland">Third Partition of Poland</a> (1795) to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/saint-petersburg">Saint Petersburg</a> as a war trophy. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/russia">Russia</a> gave several books to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-prussia">Prussia</a> in 1799, and the rest went on to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/moscow">Moscow</a>. Over 600 books are estimated to survive, with microfilms in the Lithuanian State Historical Archives in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vilnius">Vilnius</a>.</p><p>Larger collections were published from the 1830s, among them the Acts of Western Russia (1846–1853), about two thousand documents in five volumes covering 1340–1699, not all of them from the Metrica proper. Research on the Metrica itself began with Ptaszycki in 1887, and in the 1980s and 1990s a joint Belarusian, Lithuanian, Polish and Russian effort began a new wave of editions.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Lithuanian Metrica” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Ruthenian, a written East Slavic language, served as the language of the chancery, whose copybooks of charters and decisions, the <b>Lithuanian Metrica</b>, survive as a major archive.</blockquote><a href="/empire/grand-duchy-of-lithuania">Grand Duchy of Lithuania · Governance &amp; Law</a></li></ul></section>
